{
  "voice": "af_heart",
  "tailSeconds": 0.7,
  "lines": {
    "You buy 100 shares at ₹1,000 each. That is ₹1,00,000 of your money, and nothing has gone wrong yet.": {
      "file": "where-your-money-goes-01.mp3",
      "seconds": 7.467
    },
    "Weeks later you sell the same 100 shares at exactly the same price. You are square. On paper you neither made nor lost anything.": {
      "file": "where-your-money-goes-02.mp3",
      "seconds": 8.043
    },
    "You are down ₹237.82. Not because you were wrong about anything — because a trade is not free, and you paid for two of them.": {
      "file": "where-your-money-goes-03.mp3",
      "seconds": 9.152
    },
    "Here is the whole bill, biggest first. Five separate parties took a slice, and only the first one is negotiable.": {
      "file": "where-your-money-goes-04.mp3",
      "seconds": 6.891
    },
    "The largest single line is STT — ₹200.00 of the ₹237.82. It is a tax on the transaction happening, not on you doing well. You pay it in full on a trade you lost money on.": {
      "file": "where-your-money-goes-05.mp3",
      "seconds": 14.123
    },
    "If you have ever bought property, you have already paid this tax under another name. The registration charge is not a fee for a service and it is not a share of your profit — the state takes it because a transfer happened. STT is the same instrument pointed at shares: ₹200.00 on this round trip, taken on the way out, owed just as fully on the day you sell at a loss.": {
      "file": "where-your-money-goes-06.mp3",
      "seconds": 21.76
    },
    "Now look at what is missing from that bill. Brokerage is the estate agent's commission here — the one negotiable line — and on this trade it is ₹0.00. The advert was telling the truth. You paid no brokerage whatsoever, and the trade still cost you ₹237.82, because the only charge they were ever able to waive was the smallest thing on the list.": {
      "file": "where-your-money-goes-07.mp3",
      "seconds": 22.485
    },
    "Only brokerage is yours to shop around for. STT, stamp duty, the exchange charge, the regulator fee and the depository charge are the same whichever broker you use — which is why \"zero brokerage\" is an advert, not a free trade.": {
      "file": "where-your-money-goes-08.mp3",
      "seconds": 15.424
    },
    "So the price has to rise ₹2.38 per share — 0.24% — before you have made your first rupee. That is the real starting line, and no app draws it on your screen.": {
      "file": "where-your-money-goes-09.mp3",
      "seconds": 12.032
    },
    "Think of a taxi that charges fifty rupees the moment you sit down. A two-minute ride is terrible value; an hour-long one barely notices it. Traders assume costs work that way and that trading bigger dilutes them. Here they do not: almost every line on this bill is a percentage, so the starting line stays 0.24% away whether you trade a lakh or a crore.": {
      "file": "where-your-money-goes-10.mp3",
      "seconds": 22.528
    },
    "And it changes with how you trade, not just how much. The same ₹1,00,000 sold the same day is charged ₹52.34 on the sell leg instead — different product, different tax treatment, same shares.": {
      "file": "where-your-money-goes-11.mp3",
      "seconds": 13.696
    },
    "You tap buy. What leaves your phone is an instruction, not a purchase — and an instruction can be refused, delayed, or filled at a price you did not expect.": {
      "file": "what-happens-when-you-press-buy-01.mp3",
      "seconds": 9.493
    },
    "Your broker checks it first — enough money, allowed quantity, a price the exchange will accept. Plenty of orders die right here, and the reason you get back is usually one line long.": {
      "file": "what-happens-when-you-press-buy-02.mp3",
      "seconds": 10.944
    },
    "At the exchange your order joins two facing queues. Everyone wanting to buy on one side, everyone wanting to sell on the other, each holding up a price and a quantity.": {
      "file": "what-happens-when-you-press-buy-03.mp3",
      "seconds": 10.091
    },
    "The gap between the best buyer and the best seller is the spread. It is a real cost that appears on no bill: buy and instantly sell, and you are down that gap before anything has moved.": {
      "file": "what-happens-when-you-press-buy-04.mp3",
      "seconds": 10.923
    },
    "The clearest version of the spread is the currency counter at an airport. It buys dollars at one figure and sells them at a higher one, and the gap is the whole business. Walk up, change rupees to dollars and immediately change them back, and you get less money than you started with — nobody cheated you and nobody handed you a bill. Every share you buy works exactly like that.": {
      "file": "what-happens-when-you-press-buy-05.mp3",
      "seconds": 21.653
    },
    "Ask for 500 at whatever it costs and you do not get 500 at ₹1,000. You get the 180 sitting there, then the next 320 higher up. Your average is worse than the price you saw, and the bigger your order the worse it gets.": {
      "file": "what-happens-when-you-press-buy-06.mp3",
      "seconds": 15.467
    },
    "This is the part almost nobody thinks about, and it is the reason the whole thing works. When you buy a flat, you do not hand a stranger a suitcase of cash and hope the papers turn up — the money and the deeds both go to a neutral third party that releases each only once the other has arrived. The market has exactly that, for every trade, automatically.": {
      "file": "what-happens-when-you-press-buy-07.mp3",
      "seconds": 21.333
    },
    "Filled is not finished. Neither side hands anything to the other directly — money and shares both go to a clearing corporation in the middle, which is what stops your trade depending on a stranger keeping their word.": {
      "file": "what-happens-when-you-press-buy-08.mp3",
      "seconds": 12.565
    },
    "The shares land in your account the next working day. They are yours from the moment you were filled — but until the paperwork lands, some things you might want to do with them are not available yet.": {
      "file": "what-happens-when-you-press-buy-09.mp3",
      "seconds": 10.688
    },
    "You pay ₹35.52 for the right to buy at ₹990 when the share is ₹1,000, four weeks out. That one figure is really two things stuck together, and only one of them survives to the end.": {
      "file": "why-your-option-expired-worthless-01.mp3",
      "seconds": 14.208
    },
    "Before the arithmetic, the thing itself. You have seen a builder take a token to hold a flat for a month at a fixed figure. If prices jump you buy at the old figure and you have done very well. If you change your mind you lose the token and nothing more. What you bought was not the flat — it was a month of being allowed to decide, and ₹35.52 is what that month cost.": {
      "file": "why-your-option-expired-worthless-02.mp3",
      "seconds": 22.357
    },
    "Split apart: ₹10.00 of it is plain arithmetic — the ₹1,000 share against the ₹990 you agreed to pay. The other ₹25.52 is not arithmetic at all.": {
      "file": "why-your-option-expired-worthless-03.mp3",
      "seconds": 13.525
    },
    "That second ₹25.52 is what everyone else will pay you for the fact that anything could still happen before the clock runs out. Nothing has to go wrong for it to shrink. The clock alone does it.": {
      "file": "why-your-option-expired-worthless-04.mp3",
      "seconds": 12.459
    },
    "The half that melts is the one worth understanding, and there is an everyday object that behaves identically: a carton of milk. Two weeks out nobody hesitates. On the last day it is discounted hard. One day past the date it is not worth slightly less, it is worth nothing, and no shop gives you a grace period. Your ₹25.52 of time value is on exactly that schedule.": {
      "file": "why-your-option-expired-worthless-05.mp3",
      "seconds": 23.552
    },
    "Here is the melting half alone, week by week, with the share price deliberately held completely still at ₹1,000. It does not fall in a straight line — it goes slowly, then all at once, and the last week takes more than the first two put together.": {
      "file": "why-your-option-expired-worthless-06.mp3",
      "seconds": 15.275
    },
    "So being right is not enough — you have to be right by more than ₹35.52 before the clock runs out. A share that ends at ₹1,005 rose, exactly as you said, and still hands you ₹15.00 against the ₹35.52 you paid.": {
      "file": "why-your-option-expired-worthless-07.mp3",
      "seconds": 17.515
    },
    "Every figure in this explainer is a model price, not a quote — the same Black-Scholes maths the greeks widget uses, with volatility and rates held fixed. Real premiums move for reasons this deliberately leaves out. The shape of the melt is the part that is true.": {
      "file": "why-your-option-expired-worthless-08.mp3",
      "seconds": 16.107
    },
    "Every Indian stock trades inside a fence drawn before the day starts. Yesterday it closed at ₹1,000; today it is allowed anywhere between ₹900.00 and ₹1,100.00 and nowhere else. Not \"unlikely to\" — not allowed to. The exchange will refuse an order outside it.": {
      "file": "why-you-could-not-sell-01.mp3",
      "seconds": 19.221
    },
    "Now bad news arrives and the price falls all the way to the bottom of the fence, ₹900.00. Everybody who wants out today is looking at the same number, because there is no lower number to look at. This is what people mean when they say a stock is at its lower circuit.": {
      "file": "why-you-could-not-sell-02.mp3",
      "seconds": 16.789
    },
    "Look at the queues and the problem becomes obvious. Every seller in the market is stacked on one side. The other side is empty — not thin, empty. A trade needs two people, and today there is only one kind of person.": {
      "file": "why-you-could-not-sell-03.mp3",
      "seconds": 13.163
    },
    "The comparison people reach for is a referee stopping play, and it is a good one as far as it goes. The crowd is out of hand, so everything pauses until it calms down. What the comparison hides is who is standing where when the whistle blows — because the pause is not neutral. It costs whoever needed the next few seconds most.": {
      "file": "why-you-could-not-sell-04.mp3",
      "seconds": 19.712
    },
    "Here is where the money goes. Your stop-loss did everything it promised: it watched, it triggered, it sent a sell order. It just arrived in a market with nobody on the other side. A stop-loss is a request to trade, and a request needs somebody to accept it.": {
      "file": "why-you-could-not-sell-05.mp3",
      "seconds": 16.256
    },
    "So the honest way to hold a stop-loss is as a plan that usually works, not a floor under your losses. It protects you on ordinary days, which is most days. The days it fails are exactly the days you needed it — and that is not a flaw in your broker, it is what a fence around the day does.": {
      "file": "why-you-could-not-sell-06.mp3",
      "seconds": 17.515
    },
    "Almost everyone answers \"how much should I buy\" with a rupee amount they are comfortable spending. That is the wrong number. What you put in and what you can lose are two different figures, and only the second one should decide the first.": {
      "file": "how-much-should-you-buy-01.mp3",
      "seconds": 13.312
    },
    "The gap between those two bars is the whole idea, and it exists because you have a stop-loss. You are not betting ₹2,50,000 on this company being right. You are betting ₹5,000 on it not falling 2% first — and if it does, you leave. Everything else is still yours.": {
      "file": "how-much-should-you-buy-02.mp3",
      "seconds": 17.515
    },
    "Once the risk is fixed, the quantity is not a decision — it falls out. Divide what you are willing to lose by how far away your stop is. Same ₹5,000 of risk, three different stop distances, three completely different order sizes.": {
      "file": "how-much-should-you-buy-03.mp3",
      "seconds": 14.869
    },
    "Notice what that means. A tighter stop lets you buy more, not less — 250 shares at a 2% stop against 50 at 10%. The risk is identical in every case. That is the entire trick, and it is why \"how confident am I\" is not one of the inputs.": {
      "file": "how-much-should-you-buy-04.mp3",
      "seconds": 16.96
    },
    "Everything above assumed a 1% risk budget. Here is what that choice is really for. Ten losing trades in a row happens to everyone eventually — this is what is left of the account afterwards at four different budgets.": {
      "file": "how-much-should-you-buy-05.mp3",
      "seconds": 13.653
    },
    "And the part that finishes people off is the climb back, because losses and gains are not symmetrical. After ten losses at 1% you need 11% to be whole again. At 10% you need 187%. The small number is not timidity — it is the only version where being wrong ten times is survivable.": {
      "file": "how-much-should-you-buy-06.mp3",
      "seconds": 20.203
    },
    "Which is why the risk budget is decided once, while nothing is happening, and then left alone. It is not a view about this trade. Every argument about whether a particular stock is a good idea sits downstream of a number you should already have chosen — and the moment you raise it because you feel sure, you have replaced the arithmetic with a feeling.": {
      "file": "how-much-should-you-buy-07.mp3",
      "seconds": 19.904
    },
    "A pattern is announced: it works 54% of the time. That sounds like an edge until you ask the only question that matters — how often would you have been right by guessing \"up\" every single day? Suppose the answer is 52%. The pattern is now worth 2 percentage points, not 54.": {
      "file": "why-the-backtest-lied-01.mp3",
      "seconds": 18.411
    },
    "Medicine has known this for a century and never states a result without it. Nobody accepts \"eighty percent of patients improved\" on its own, because the first question back is always how many would have improved anyway. A market has a base rate too, and almost nothing published about patterns quotes it.": {
      "file": "why-the-backtest-lied-02.mp3",
      "seconds": 17.643
    },
    "This is the part that does the damage, and it is pure arithmetic. Take an idea with no merit at all and test it at the usual 5% threshold: a 1 in 20 chance it looks significant by luck. Now test 5 ideas, then 20, then 100. The chance that at least one worthless idea passes climbs to 99%.": {
      "file": "why-the-backtest-lied-03.mp3",
      "seconds": 21.013
    },
    "Nobody sets out to do this. You try a 20-day average, then 50, then 200; you try it on large caps, then mid caps; you add a volume filter. That is dozens of tests, and you only ever report the one that worked. The result is not the best rule — it is the luckiest one, and luck does not repeat.": {
      "file": "why-the-backtest-lied-04.mp3",
      "seconds": 19.221
    },
    "A tailor fits a suit to one body. Every extra measurement makes it fit that person better and everybody else worse, and past a point the suit is a description of one afternoon rather than a garment. A rule tuned until it fits ten years of history exactly has become a description of those ten years.": {
      "file": "why-the-backtest-lied-05.mp3",
      "seconds": 18.752
    },
    "The first is using information that had not arrived yet. It happens by accident constantly: you decide to buy on days that closed strong, and record the trade at that day's open. The decision used the close. The trade used the open. On the day itself, the close had not happened.": {
      "file": "why-the-backtest-lied-06.mp3",
      "seconds": 17.557
    },
    "The second is subtler and it is baked into most data. Test a strategy on the fifty companies in the index today, and every company that was in it and got thrown out is missing from your sample. You have tested it exclusively on survivors, and survivors are not a random selection.": {
      "file": "why-the-backtest-lied-07.mp3",
      "seconds": 16.768
    },
    "And when a rule does survive all of that, judge it on expectancy rather than on how often it is right. These three are the same idea reached three ways: being right seldom and large beats being right often and small, and a high hit rate can be the worst of the set.": {
      "file": "why-the-backtest-lied-08.mp3",
      "seconds": 15.509
    },
    "A candle is not a picture of a day. It is four numbers from a day, arranged into a shape: where it opened, where it closed, and the furthest it got in each direction. The thick part is open-to-close. The thin line is everything else that happened.": {
      "file": "what-one-candle-hides-01.mp3",
      "seconds": 14.763
    },
    "Now read what those four numbers actually say. The price covered 8.00 points of ground between its lowest and highest moment, and finished 1.00 above where it started. Almost everything that happened cancelled out — and the shape shows you the 1.00, not the 8.00.": {
      "file": "what-one-candle-hides-02.mp3",
      "seconds": 19.051
    },
    "Here is the part that matters for every rule anyone builds on candles. This one is consistent with a day that opened, sank to 98 first while everyone panicked, then climbed all the way to 106 before easing back.": {
      "file": "what-one-candle-hides-03.mp3",
      "seconds": 13.888
    },
    "It is equally consistent with the exact opposite: a day that ran to 106 in the first hour, gave all of it back and more, and only recovered to 101 at the very end. Two completely different days. One identical candle. Nothing in the shape distinguishes them.": {
      "file": "what-one-candle-hides-04.mp3",
      "seconds": 17.195
    },
    "Which is why a candle is best thought of as a weather summary rather than a recording. High of thirty-four, low of nineteen, ended mild. True, useful, and it cannot tell you whether the cold came before the heat — and if your plan depended on being outside at the right moment, that is the only thing you needed.": {
      "file": "what-one-candle-hides-05.mp3",
      "seconds": 17.963
    },
    "Volume gets read the same hopeful way. It counts how many shares changed hands and nothing else — not who was keen, not which side was in a hurry. Every share sold was also bought, so a heavy day cannot tell you that selling outweighed buying. It only tells you a lot of people disagreed about the price.": {
      "file": "what-one-candle-hides-06.mp3",
      "seconds": 18.091
    },
    "So use the candle for what it holds — four honest numbers — and stop asking it for the fifth. If your rule depends on which came first, the high or the low, it depends on something this data does not contain, and testing it on this data will not find that out.": {
      "file": "what-one-candle-hides-07.mp3",
      "seconds": 15.296
    }
  }
}
